 ## Purpose and scope
 
-This standard holds the conventions of Julia source that the language-neutral practice of STND.CODE and STND.DOCS leaves to the language. They cover the version of Julia that the work tracks, its bounds, checks, manifests, and run records (STND.JL.1 to STND.JL.5); names (STND.JL.6 and STND.JL.7); interfaces between packages (STND.JL.8, STND.JL.9, and STND.JL.10); types that the compiler infers (STND.JL.11 and STND.JL.12); failures (STND.JL.13 to STND.JL.18); arithmetic state and comparisons (STND.JL.19, STND.JL.20, and STND.JL.21); random numbers (STND.JL.22 to STND.JL.26); state shared between tasks (STND.JL.27); docstrings and doctests (STND.JL.28 to STND.JL.31); and the checks that confirm them at each change (STND.JL.32 to STND.JL.35). Its basis is the reference manual, the Julia manual of the reference version. STND.JL.13 gives STND.CODE.6, and STND.JL.14 and STND.JL.15 give STND.CODE.7, their forms in Julia's constructs; STND.JL.16 and STND.JL.21 apply STND.CODE.12 and STND.CODE.10 to Julia's exceptions and tolerances. STND.JL.13, STND.JL.14, and STND.JL.15 instantiate PRIME.4, so they are binding and apply at every class; every other provision is a default (GOV.19) and a recommendation, which a project replaces by a recorded decision with an alternative that meets the same higher outcome (REPRO.7, PRIN.10). The provisions name Julia's constructs and files and no version, package, or tool; the repository configuration of each work selects its versions and tools (GOV.18).
